## The plan of this paragraph

This block is a plan.
It is not a draft of the paragraph.
Each sentence in it only says what is meant.
None of its wording is kept.
You choose every word of the paragraph.
One thing keeps one name through the whole paragraph.
{{#if plan}}

### What the paragraph says

{{#each plan.arguments}}
- Argument {{number}}. {{sentence}}
{{/each}}
- CentralClaim. {{plan.central_claim}}

The reader must be able to recover every Argument from the paragraph.
The reader must be able to recover the CentralClaim from the paragraph.
{{#if plan.segments}}

### The parts of the paragraph, in order

Each part is about one sentence.
A part that names an Argument says that Argument.
A part that names the CentralClaim says the CentralClaim.
A part that names neither adds no fact.
A line may say how its part stands to an earlier part.
It names that earlier part before the colon.
The text after the colon speaks of two sentences only, this part and the part named before the colon.
In that text, "this sentence" is this part.
In that text, "the sentence pointed to" is the part named before the colon.
Where a line names several earlier parts, each text holds on its own for the part it names.
It says nothing of how those earlier parts stand to each other.
A part that later parts point to also does what each of their lines says of the sentence pointed to.

